        "math_academy_id": "q-86185",
        "problem": "Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{2}+15x}{(x-4)(x+2)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{(x-4)}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)},\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B$ and $C$ are real constants, calculate $A+B+C.$",
        "worked_solution": "Our task is to find $A,$ $B$ and $C$ such that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{x}^{2}+15x}{(x-4)(x+2)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{(x-4)}+\\frac{B}{(x+2)}+\\frac{C}{(2x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-4)(x+2)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}+15x=A(x+2)(2x+1)+B(x-4)(2x+1)+C(x-4)(x+2).\n$$\n\nFor $x=-2,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(-2)^{2}+15(-2) & =A(-2+2)(2(-2)+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(-2-4)(2(-2)+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(-2-4)(-2+2) \\\\ 12-30 & =B(-6)(-3) \\\\ -18 & =18B \\\\ B & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=4,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(4)^{2}+15(4) & =A(4+2)(2(4)+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(4-4)(2(4)+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(4-4)(4+2) \\\\ 48+60 & =A(6)(9) \\\\ 108 & =54A \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+15(-\\frac{1}{2}) & =A(-\\frac{1}{2}+2)(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\  & \\,+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-4)(2(-\\frac{1}{2})+1) \\\\  & \\,+C(-\\frac{1}{2}-4)(-\\frac{1}{2}+2) \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{15}{2} & =C(-\\frac{9}{2})(\\frac{3}{2}) \\\\ -\\frac{27}{4} & =-\\frac{27}{4}C \\\\ C & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+B+C & =2+(-1)+1=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
